<BaseLayout title="What to put in a website brief | Wear Valley Digital" description="A practical checklist for agreeing customer journeys, content, integrations and acceptance before a website build." canonical="https://wearvalleydigital.com/insights/website-brief/" disableAnalytics={true}>
<section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Buyer guide</p><h1>What to put in a website brief</h1><p class="lede">Start with what people need to do. The technology choice follows from those requirements.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><article class="wrap prose"><nav class="guide-contents" aria-label="In this guide"><h2>In this guide</h2><ol><li><a href="#describe-one-useful-customer-journey">Describe one useful customer journey</a></li><li><a href="#list-the-content-and-who-maintains-it">List the content and who maintains it</a></li><li><a href="#make-integrations-explicit">Make integrations explicit</a></li><li><a href="#agree-how-you-will-accept-the-work">Agree how you will accept the work</a></li><li><a href="#plan-the-handover">Plan the handover</a></li><li><a href="#bring-uncertainty-to-the-conversation">Bring uncertainty to the conversation</a></li></ol></nav><p>By Wear Valley Digital · Updated <time datetime="2026-10-02">2 October 2026</time></p><h2 id="describe-one-useful-customer-journey" tabindex="-1">Describe one useful customer journey</h2><p>Write down who the site is for and their next action. A restaurant visitor might need the menu, opening hours and a way to request a table. A salon customer might need to choose a treatment and find an appointment.</p><p>A request that someone confirms is different from a booking against live availability. State which behaviour you need, who handles the result and what the customer should see if it fails.</p><h2 id="list-the-content-and-who-maintains-it" tabindex="-1">List the content and who maintains it</h2><p>List the pages, images, menus or price lists you already have. Identify missing material and who will provide it. Decide which information your team needs to change and who can approve those changes.</p><h2 id="make-integrations-explicit" tabindex="-1">Make integrations explicit</h2><p>Name existing calendars, booking tools or business systems that the site must use. Ask who owns each account, what access is available and whether the connection has a usage or licence cost. An integration shown in a demo is not evidence that your account is connected.</p><h2 id="agree-how-you-will-accept-the-work" tabindex="-1">Agree how you will accept the work</h2><p>Use examples that can be checked: finding the contact details on a phone, completing an enquiry, receiving a clear confirmation and reaching the right person. Record what is included, what is excluded and what happens when the brief changes.</p><h2 id="plan-the-handover" tabindex="-1">Plan the handover</h2><p>Ask who controls the domain, hosting and business accounts; who can export the content and data; and what is needed to move the site. Agree maintenance and recovery responsibilities alongside the build.</p><h2 id="bring-uncertainty-to-the-conversation" tabindex="-1">Bring uncertainty to the conversation</h2><p>You do not need every answer before an initial discussion. Share the problem, existing site or workflow, timing constraints and any budget context you know. Where a requirement is unclear, agree how it will be investigated before committing to a build.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button" href="/services/">Explore WVD services</a><a class="text-link" href="/products/">Explore products in development →</a></div><p><a href="/insights/">Back to buyer guides</a></p></article></section>
</BaseLayout>
